  "casanova": {
    title: "Casanova",
    videoID: "gNZg9PvZBFU",
    description: "<p>Casanova is a short documentary centered on the life of wrestler Johnny Casanova, produced in the spring of 2025 as part of a senior-level documentary course. The project began with a broad and somewhat uncertain scope. I initially reached out to numerous wrestlers online with the intention of capturing a range of voices and experiences within the wrestling community. This outreach process was both practical and exploratory. It allowed me to secure access to an event where multiple wrestlers would be present, but it also reflected an openness in my approach. At that stage, I was not committed to a single protagonist. Instead, I was interested in observing, listening, and discovering where the most compelling story might emerge.<p/><p>The entire film was shot in a single day, which required a high level of preparation but also adaptability. Going into the shoot, I had to think carefully about how to maximize coverage by capturing interviews, candid moments, and environmental details while working within tight time constraints. Shooting solo on a Sony a6700 with just a kit lens meant I had to make constant decisions about framing, exposure, and movement without the luxury of a larger crew or multiple setups. This limitation also shaped the film’s aesthetic. It encouraged a more intimate, observational style and forced me to stay close to my subjects, both physically and emotionally. With minimal production support from a fellow student, the process became a balance between technical execution and being present enough to recognize meaningful moments as they unfolded.<p/><p>It was not until the editing phase that the film truly found its direction. Reviewing the footage, I began to notice that Johnny Casanova’s story had a depth and emotional resonance that distinguished it from the others. This reflects a common documentary process. Rather than imposing a narrative from the start, the story reveals itself through patterns in the material. Casanova’s experiences carried a humanistic quality that aligned with the kinds of stories I am most drawn to, which are personal, reflective, and grounded in lived experience rather than spectacle. Choosing to center the film around him required reshaping the entire structure, recontextualizing other interviews, and sometimes letting go of footage that was interesting but did not serve the central narrative.<p/><p>The editing process itself was one of the most challenging aspects of the project. Because the footage was captured in a single day without a predetermined storyline, constructing a cohesive and engaging arc required careful selection and sequencing. I had to think about pacing, thematic continuity, and emotional progression, considering how each moment leads into the next and builds toward a larger understanding of Casanova as both a wrestler and a person. This often meant experimenting with different structures and testing how voiceover, interview clips, and observational footage could interweave to create a natural flow. The difficulty was not just technical. It was also conceptual, requiring me to determine what the film was really about and how to express that clearly.<p/><p>I also took on part of the film’s score, composing music for one section to help shape its tone and emotional rhythm. This added another layer of authorship and allowed me to directly influence how the audience experiences certain moments. However, time constraints limited how far I could take this aspect. With more time, I would have expanded the score, particularly for the final sequence, to create a stronger sense of closure and cohesion.<p/><p>Overall, Casanova reflects a process that moved from openness and exploration to focus and intentionality. It demonstrates how documentary filmmaking often relies on discovery, both in the field and in the edit, and how limitations in time, equipment, and structure can shape the creative outcome in meaningful ways.</p>",
    year: "2025",
    type: "Short Documentary",
    role: "Director, Cinematographer, Editor"
    },
